WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.267
This provided script loads sample records into the clinic schema.

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:07.672
Some of the SQL uses lookup and join patterns that are taught in more detail later,

00:00:07.672 --> 00:00:10.512
so you do not need to write this loading script from memory.

00:00:10.512 --> 00:00:13.247
Run it in the browser and inspect the final result.

00:00:13.247 --> 00:00:18.982
Focus on what is happening conceptually: patients, providers, and services are inserted first.

00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:22.177
The appointment refers to existing patient and provider rows.

00:00:22.177 --> 00:00:25.307
Appointment-service rows then connect the appointment to services.

00:00:25.307 --> 00:00:28.367
The insert order follows the relationships we designed.

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:30.142
If you use pgAdmin, run the schema first and then the loading script.

00:00:30.142 --> 00:00:34.452
Parent data must exist before child rows that reference it can be inserted.

00:00:34.452 --> 00:00:39.122
Connect this idea to a university system separating students, courses, sections, and enrollments.

00:00:39.122 --> 00:00:43.127
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:43.127 --> 00:00:48.037
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:48.037 --> 00:00:51.952
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:56.480
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
